1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,727
Can this robot do my job?

2
00:00:02,727 --> 00:00:10,000
Today you often find out after you buy it, and then you teach it from zero.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,846
So I built Translator.

4
00:00:11,846 --> 00:00:14,154
You film a job once.

5
00:00:14,154 --> 00:00:18,308
This stock video shows a hand and a lemon.

6
00:00:18,308 --> 00:00:22,000
SAM 3.1 tracks the objects and the hand.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:31,100
Muse Spark watches the video and writes a skill card: the goal, the objects, the grip, the steps, the success test.

8
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:33,267
The meaning, not the motion.

9
00:00:33,267 --> 00:00:35,000
56 seconds, 7 cents.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,632
Here is another stock video: a box, moved with two hands.

11
00:00:39,632 --> 00:00:43,421
Each robot checks the card against its own body.

12
00:00:43,421 --> 00:00:47,632
The box is 146 millimetres wide, this gripper opens 85.

13
00:00:47,632 --> 00:00:51,000
So it says no, with a measured reason.

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,500
For the lemon, most robots say yes.

15
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:58,000
Same card, each in its own way.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:07,758
They also open a door, and the G1 carries a crate with two hands; here the base slides, the legs do not step.

17
00:01:07,758 --> 00:01:12,000
Nine robots, eleven skills, 80 percent of 760 tries passed.

18
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,833
Because the card holds the goal, not a path, you can mess with it.

19
00:01:17,833 --> 00:01:20,750
Here a stick knocks the cube away.

20
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:23,250
A replay carries on with nothing.

21
00:01:23,250 --> 00:01:27,000
This robot looks again and goes back for it.

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:35,333
Across six robots and scripted moves and hits on the cube, it passed 372 of 420 tries; replay passed 49.

23
00:01:35,333 --> 00:01:43,250
A simple re-plan from the card does a little better, 398, because the card is what makes recovery possible.

24
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:47,000
Try it in your browser, the link is below.

25
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,913
Muse reads the model file and writes the adapter.

26
00:01:50,913 --> 00:01:56,565
If a test fails, Muse gets the numbers and frames and fixes it.

27
00:01:56,565 --> 00:02:04,391
The Franka FR3 took 21 seconds on Muse Spark Open, and passed 10 out of 10 first try.

28
00:02:04,391 --> 00:02:07,000
Of seven new robots, five passed.

29
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,783
SAM 3.1 can also referee a robot's clip from video alone.

30
00:02:11,783 --> 00:02:17,000
On 40 clean simulated clips it agreed with the simulator 39 times.

31
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,512
Everything here is simulation, no real robot yet.

32
00:02:20,512 --> 00:02:28,854
When a robot trains its own policy from cameras, it passes 38 to 74 percent, behind the planned motion.

33
00:02:28,854 --> 00:02:35,000
Watch the UR5e learn: on the same 20 layouts, 6, 6, 13, then 15.

34
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,769
Everything here was built during this hackathon, with SAM 3.1, Muse Spark and Muse Spark Open.

35
00:02:41,769 --> 00:02:43,462
Show a task once.

36
00:02:43,462 --> 00:02:46,000
See which robots can do it.
